        {
          "headline": "4B open-weight decision model in a harness confirms 187 memory-safety findings across more than 4,000 C/C++ projects",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "arXiv", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16565" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "The campaign confirmed 187 memory-safety findings; by worst-case CVSS v3.1, 12 are critical, 148 high, 23 medium and 4 low. On three GB10 systems the harness searched more than 4,000 projects at 900-1,100 repositories per hour, and the paper states the search uses no frontier model.",
            "Intern-Decision-4B, a 4B-parameter open-weight decision model, scores every candidate function in one forward pass. The authors are explicit that the model is weak alone: it separates vulnerable from benign functions with AUC 0.73, but a vulnerable function from its own patch with only 0.56. Its value is ordering candidates, not accepting or rejecting them.",
            "The decision step runs at 4.4 decisions per second on one GB10 and 9.8 on a consumer RTX 4090 gaming PC, which is the point the paper makes about cost: large-scale vulnerability search no longer requires frontier-model access.",
            "A preprint, submitted 12 November, by authors affiliated to Cyrex and Deus Rex. The authors say findings are being disclosed to maintainers under coordinated disclosure before publication, and describe one of their own headline comparisons as an observed, selection-biased upper bound."
          ],
          "topics": ["cyber-defense", "open-weights", "agent-security"],
          "storylines": ["ai-enabled-hacking"],
          "impact": "mixed",
          "flags": ["preprint", "single-source", "company-claim"]
        },
        {
          "headline": "Fabricated risk warnings push Gemini's induced over-abstention from 9.8% to 86.3% under caution-first prompting",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "arXiv", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14864" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "The paper studies warning-induced over-abstention, an availability attack in which adversaries place fabricated risk warnings in low-trust content to stop an agent completing a legitimate task. Across three model families and 51 injectable AgentAbstain task pairs, caution-first prompting amplifies the attack: for Gemini, induced over-abstention rises from 9.8% to 86.3%, despite unchanged clean-task completion.",
            "The framing is the useful part: an agent's decision not to act is itself an attack surface, and safe abstention requires evaluating both the claimed risk and the authority of the evidence supporting it.",
            "The authors evaluate a Verify-Before-Abstain protocol that corroborates warnings against authoritative records or the underlying content. They report provenance-aware verification improves legitimate completion, while forgery and sensitive-content controls expose its dependence on authoritative evidence and its limitations in preserving confidentiality.",
            "A preprint, submitted 11 November, by a single author with no institutional affiliation listed on the arXiv page. The work is a controlled benchmark; no exploitation in the wild is reported."
          ],
          "topics": ["prompt-injection", "agent-security", "agents"],
          "storylines": ["agents-going-wrong"],
          "impact": "harmful",
          "flags": ["preprint", "single-source"]
        }
