        {
          "headline": "UK inspectorate finds police facial-recognition matches not always corroborated, raising wrongful-arrest risk, across 300,000 searches a year",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "Biometric Update", "url": "https://www.biometricupdate.com/202611/uk-police-dont-know-how-much-retrospective-facial-recognition-helps-inspection" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "HM Inspectorate of Constabulary and Fire & Rescue Services published a 58-page report with 16 recommendations on how police forces and the National Crime Agency use retrospective facial recognition, commissioned by the Home Secretary in November 2025. Inspectors found that investigators did not always corroborate potential matches before taking action, increasing the risk of wrongful identification and arrest.",
            "UK law enforcement bodies make more than 300,000 retrospective facial recognition search requests per year, up from around 250,000 searches in 2024. Two forces made over 25,000 requests from April 2025 through March 2026 while several made fewer than a thousand, and forces generally could not clearly show performance improvements from using the technology.",
            "A Home Office survey published alongside it covered 779 Police National Database users, 13 percent of eligible users. Nearly two-thirds could not recall any training on facial recognition in the past year, and roughly one-third were either wrong or unsure about whether facial recognition matches meet the legal requirement for evidence of identification, which they do not.",
            "The report covers retrospective facial recognition only, not live or operator-initiated deployments. HMICFRS's own news release and the report PDF could not be opened for this edition; the figures here are as reported by Biometric Update, which quotes the report directly."
          ],
          "topics": ["surveillance", "uk", "privacy", "incidents"],
