          "storylines": ["ai-enabled-hacking"],
          "impact": "mixed",
          "flags": ["preprint", "single-source", "company-claim"]
        },
        {
          "headline": "Fabricated risk warnings push Gemini's induced over-abstention from 9.8% to 86.3% under caution-first prompting",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "arXiv", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14864" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "The paper studies warning-induced over-abstention, an availability attack in which adversaries place fabricated risk warnings in low-trust content to stop an agent completing a legitimate task. Across three model families and 51 injectable AgentAbstain task pairs, caution-first prompting amplifies the attack: for Gemini, induced over-abstention rises from 9.8% to 86.3%, despite unchanged clean-task completion.",
            "The framing is the useful part: an agent's decision not to act is itself an attack surface, and safe abstention requires evaluating both the claimed risk and the authority of the evidence supporting it.",
            "The authors evaluate a Verify-Before-Abstain protocol that corroborates warnings against authoritative records or the underlying content. They report provenance-aware verification improves legitimate completion, while forgery and sensitive-content controls expose its dependence on authoritative evidence and its limitations in preserving confidentiality.",
            "A preprint, submitted 11 November, by a single author with no institutional affiliation listed on the arXiv page. The work is a controlled benchmark; no exploitation in the wild is reported."
